{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"07","chapter_title":"GitHub Pages Personal Website","title":"Maintenance Plan","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting maintenance plan to AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website","cat100_supplement_maintenance_plan","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/06/06"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_6_7_maintenance_plan_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_6_7_maintenance_plan_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_6_7_maintenance_plan_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_6_7_maintenance_plan_calibration"]},"content":"# Maintenance Plan\n\n![Maintenance Plan textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/06-07-maintenance-plan.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA website is not finished when it goes live; it starts decaying the moment it does. Resume PDFs go stale, project links break when files are renamed, and a portfolio that proudly says \"first-year student\" two years later quietly tells visitors you stopped caring. A maintenance plan is the small, repeatable routine that keeps a published site honest over time. The hard part is not writing a plan but writing one you will actually follow: specific enough to act on, triggered by a real date, and matched to the kind of site you actually run.\n\nThis section also teaches a transferable judgment skill: filtering a generic AI-generated checklist down to what fits your situation. A static GitHub Pages site has no server to patch and no SSL certificate to renew, so a one-size-fits-all maintenance list includes tasks that are simply impossible for you. Learning to drop the irrelevant items and keep the real ones is the core move.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student finishes his portfolio and writes a maintenance note: \"Keep the site updated sometime.\" A year later nothing has changed. The resume PDF is from freshman year, two project links 404 because he renamed the files, and the About page still says \"incoming first-year.\" A recruiter who finds the site assumes it is abandoned. Wanting to do better, he asks an AI for a website maintenance checklist and gets a generic list: renew the SSL certificate monthly, patch server dependencies, refresh content quarterly, check links periodically. He starts trying to renew an SSL certificate that GitHub already manages for him and looking for a server that does not exist.\n\nTwo failures meet here: a plan too vague to follow, and a checklist not matched to a static site. This section fixes both.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA usable maintenance plan has three properties.\n\n**It is triggered, not aspirational.** \"Sometime\" never arrives. A plan needs a concrete trigger tied to the calendar: the first day of each quarter, the start of each semester, the day after every job application. A trigger turns intention into a recurring event you can actually notice.\n\n**It is specific and verifiable.** \"Keep it updated\" cannot be checked off; \"replace the oldest project card with my newest work\" can. Each step should be a discrete action a future version of you can perform without interpreting what you meant, and a reviewer can confirm was done.\n\n**It is matched to the platform.** Maintenance tasks depend on what kind of site you run. A static GitHub Pages site has no backend, so server patching and dependency updates do not apply, and Pages provides HTTPS automatically, so certificate renewal is GitHub's job, not yours. What does apply to a static portfolio is content freshness (resume, projects) and link integrity (renamed files break internal links). Filtering a generic list down to platform-relevant tasks is itself the skill.\n\n> **Recent evidence (as of 2026).** The platform claims hold: GitHub Pages still auto-provisions and auto-renews free HTTPS through Let's Encrypt for github.io addresses and correctly configured custom domains, so certificate renewal is never your task. One nuance is worth a line in your plan: all Pages sites now build and deploy through GitHub Actions, and if you publish via your own Actions workflow file, that file is a dependency you own that can break when Actions or runner versions are deprecated. For that path, \"confirm the latest deploy succeeded\" is a real platform-relevant maintenance item; for a plain deploy-from-a-branch site like this portfolio, content freshness and link integrity remain the whole job.\n\nThe table shows that filtering applied to a typical AI checklist:\n\n| AI checklist item | Keep for static GitHub Pages? | Reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Renew SSL certificate monthly | Drop | Pages provides HTTPS automatically |\n| Patch server dependencies | Drop | No server exists to patch |\n| Refresh content quarterly | Keep | Resume and projects go stale |\n| Check links periodically | Keep | Renamed files break internal links |\n\nThis is [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students) in its quiet form: maintaining what you publish is part of publishing responsibly, and a neglected site misleads the people who rely on it. [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined) frames the routine as professionalism; keeping a public artifact current is the kind of follow-through employers value.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A generic AI checklist tells you to renew the SSL certificate monthly for your static GitHub Pages portfolio. Keep this item?\" explanation=\"GitHub Pages provides HTTPS automatically, so certificate renewal is GitHub's job, not yours. Keeping the item would have you chasing a task you cannot perform. The platform check drops server and certificate tasks for a static site and keeps content freshness and link integrity.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Drop it: Pages provides HTTPS automatically, so certificate renewal is not your task\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Keep it: every website needs its certificate renewed monthly by the owner\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Keep it but change monthly to yearly to reduce the workload\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nMaria's site is live. Now she writes a maintenance plan she can actually follow, and she filters a generic AI checklist down to what fits a static GitHub Pages site.\n\n**Step 1. Start from the AI draft and judge each item.**\n\n| AI checklist item | Keep for GitHub Pages? | Reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Renew SSL certificate monthly | Drop | Pages provides HTTPS automatically |\n| Patch server dependencies | Drop | Static site; there is no server to patch |\n| Refresh content quarterly | Keep | Resume and projects go stale |\n| Check all links periodically | Keep | Renamed files break internal links |\n\n**Step 2. Turn the kept items into a triggered, specific plan.**\n\n```text\nMAINTENANCE PLAN (my-portfolio)\nTrigger: first day of each quarter (Jan 1, Apr 1, Jul 1, Oct 1)\n1. Replace the oldest project card with my newest work.\n2. Export the current resume to assets/resume.pdf.\n3. Click every nav link and every project link; fix any 404.\n4. Open the live URL on a phone to confirm layout still holds.\n```\n\n**Step 3. Record why a vague version was rejected.** The first draft said \"keep the site updated sometime.\" That has no trigger and no task list, so it can never be verified or repeated. Naming the date and the four steps makes it actionable.\n\nThe plan now matches the platform (no server tasks), names a concrete cadence, and lists steps a future Maria can perform without guessing. Maintenance became a repeatable routine instead of a good intention.\n\nOrder the steps that turn a generic AI checklist into a triggered, platform-fit routine. Filter for the platform before you write any steps, and add the trigger before you call it a plan.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Start from the AI checklist and run the platform check on each item\",\"Drop server patching and SSL renewal: a static site has neither\",\"Keep content freshness and link integrity: these apply to a static site\",\"Rewrite each kept item as a specific verifiable action\",\"Attach a concrete trigger such as the first day of each quarter\",\"Record why the vague keep it updated sometime draft was rejected\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nTest any maintenance plan against three questions before you call it done:\n\n1. **Trigger check:** Is there a concrete date or event that starts the routine? If the answer is \"when I get around to it,\" the plan will not run.\n2. **Specificity check:** Can each step be performed without reinterpreting it and confirmed as done? Replace verbs like \"update\" with named actions like \"export resume to assets/resume.pdf.\"\n3. **Platform check:** Does every task actually apply to a static GitHub Pages site? Drop server and certificate tasks; keep content and link tasks.\n\nA plan that passes all three is a routine. A plan that fails the trigger check is a wish, and one that fails the platform check sends you chasing problems you do not have.\n\nWalk the maintenance note Keep the site updated sometime through the three checks and predict each verdict before you reveal it. The site is a static GitHub Pages portfolio.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Test the vague note with the trigger, specificity, and platform checks\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Trigger check first: is there a concrete date or event that starts the routine?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes, sometime counts as a schedule\",\"No, sometime never arrives, so the plan will never run\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Sometime is not a trigger. Without a concrete date or event such as the first day of each quarter, the routine never actually fires.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Specificity check next: can keep the site updated be performed and confirmed as done?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes, you just update whatever needs it\",\"No, it cannot be checked off; it needs named actions\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Updated is not a verifiable step. Replace it with named actions like export the current resume to assets/resume.pdf and click every link, fixing any 404.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Verdict: the note fails the trigger and specificity checks (the platform check is moot until there are real tasks). Rewrite it as a dated routine with named, checkable steps so a future you can run it cold.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common misreading is that more thorough means better. A long checklist copied from a generic source feels responsible but wastes effort on impossible tasks (patching a nonexistent server) and buries the two items that matter (content and links). Relevance beats length.\n\nA second misreading is treating \"I'll keep it updated\" as a plan. Without a trigger and named steps, it is a hope. The discipline is to convert intention into a dated, checkable routine.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the rule to the student's situation. He starts from the AI checklist and runs the platform check first. \"Renew SSL certificate monthly\" is dropped because Pages handles HTTPS automatically. \"Patch server dependencies\" is dropped because a static site has no server. \"Refresh content quarterly\" and \"check links periodically\" survive, because resumes go stale and renamed files break links on exactly this kind of site.\n\nHe then runs the specificity and trigger checks by rewriting the survivors into a dated routine: trigger on the first day of each quarter, then four named steps (replace the oldest project card, export the current resume to assets/resume.pdf, click every nav and project link and fix any 404, open the live URL on a phone to confirm layout). Compared with \"keep the site updated sometime,\" every step is now actionable and verifiable, and the cadence is concrete. The plan matches the platform, names a trigger, and lists steps a future version of him can follow without guessing.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader can take any generic maintenance item and decide instantly whether it applies to a static site and how to make it specific. As you study the worked example, cover the \"Keep?\" column and judge each AI item yourself, then cover the routine and try to rewrite a vague task into a verifiable one. Matching the table and the routine means you can build a plan, not just copy one.\n\nA strong reader of maintenance planning can answer four questions:\n\n1. What trigger starts this plan, and is it concrete enough to notice?\n2. Which generic checklist items are impossible on a static site, and why?\n3. Could a stranger follow each step without asking what you meant?\n4. How would you confirm, weeks later, that the routine actually ran?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour maintenance plan is strong when a future version of you, returning after months away, could execute it cold: a clear trigger, named steps, nothing that requires interpretation, and nothing that applies to a platform you do not run. The plan should read like a short procedure, not a paragraph of good intentions.\n\nA strong submission names one item you deliberately dropped: \"I removed 'renew SSL monthly' because GitHub Pages provides HTTPS automatically, so the task is not mine to do and including it would only waste time each quarter.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same planning discipline to a different artifact: maintaining a shared team spreadsheet instead of a website. The platform changes, so the specific tasks change (archive last quarter's tab, re-verify the source data link, confirm formulas still reference the right ranges), but the three properties hold. The plan still needs a trigger (end of each month), still needs verifiable steps (not \"tidy it up\" but \"delete rows older than one year\"), and still must match the artifact (a spreadsheet has no SSL, but it has formula integrity). Carrying trigger, specificity, and platform-fit to a non-web artifact is the proof you learned to design a maintenance routine rather than memorize a website checklist.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/06-07-maintenance-plan-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"06.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions for CAT 100 06.07. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_maintenance_plan\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links claim, evidence, tool boundary, revision, and remaining limitation. That is what separates digital fluency from simple completion.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_maintenance_plan\", \"cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website\", \"evidence_based_revision\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_7_maintenance_plan_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_maintenance_plan","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_maintenance_plan","cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website"],"stem":"Which item belongs in a realistic maintenance plan for a personal portfolio site?","options":["A promise to redesign the entire site every week","A recurring quarterly check to update the resume PDF, swap in the newest project, and test that all links still resolve","Nothing; a published site never needs attention","Deleting the site after one month so it cannot go stale"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Maintenance is small and recurring: refresh content, add new work, and re-test links on a set cadence. Weekly redesigns and never touching it are both unrealistic.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_7_publish_and_forget","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_7_maintenance_plan_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_maintenance_plan","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_maintenance_plan"],"stem":"Six months after launch, a visitor clicks your Projects link and gets a 404 because you renamed the file. What maintenance practice would have caught this?","options":["Hoping no one notices","A periodic link check (manual click-through or a link-checker tool) listed in the maintenance plan","Removing the navigation bar entirely","Making the site password protected"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Renaming or moving files breaks internal links. A scheduled link check, manual or automated, is the maintenance step that catches broken paths before visitors do.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_7_links_never_break","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_7_maintenance_plan_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat100_supplement_maintenance_plan"],"stem":"You ask an AI to generate a maintenance checklist. It includes 'renew your SSL certificate monthly' and 'update server dependencies.' Your site is a static GitHub Pages site. What is the right response?","options":["Follow all items literally; the AI knows best","Drop the items that do not apply to static GitHub Pages (it manages HTTPS and has no server dependencies) and keep the items that do, like content and link checks","Stop using GitHub Pages because it cannot do those things","Add even more server tasks to be safe"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A generic AI checklist assumes a server you do not run. GitHub Pages handles HTTPS automatically and has no dependencies to patch, so you keep only the items that fit a static site.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_7_generic_ai_checklist_applied","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_7_maintenance_plan_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_maintenance_plan","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_maintenance_plan","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Which maintenance-plan entry is specific enough to actually act on later?","options":["Keep the site updated sometime","On the first of each quarter: replace the oldest project card, refresh resume.pdf, and click every nav and project link","Make it good","Update when I feel like it"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An actionable plan names the trigger (first of each quarter) and the exact tasks. Vague intentions like sometime or make it good cannot be verified or repeated.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_7_vague_plan_is_a_plan","difficulty":"medium"}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"df0707044566db5c8b22ca4922bffebc6e8b2cf9247582b453905a82ba7f59ee","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}